 the extraordinary political  and economic evolution beginning to  occur from Japan and Korea to singapore  and china remained a curious but very  closed society and almost no reporting  came out of it at home rock and roll was  treated if at all on the evening news is  a curiosity and not as the transforming  cultural event of the time the changing  role of the young is represented by the  largest most vigorous generation in the  history of mankind the baby boomers was  little notice outside of the political  protest context and it was a world a  prism if you will reflecting the  interest of people who made those  decisions all of whom happened to be  white middle-aged men from a common  culture along the eastern seaboard they  were serious professionals of  unquestioned integrity and intelligence  but they understandably reflected the  sensibilities of their time and place  they were my role models and they were  my mentors after all I aspired to be in  their place and I share their interest  as someone who expected to be a white  middle-aged man myself one day I was  content to follow their lead as I set  out of my own pilgrimage now back to the  future if I were that same teenager in  South Dakota and remember I made these  remarks in 2001 at Harvard I would have  access to three full throat  Networks three full-time cable news  channels and a local news channel as  well of two financial news cable  channels three sports networks a history  channel biography channel two cable  channels broadcasting public policy  discussions all day every day a  wide-ranging public broadcasting system  with its own evening news and an  award-winning documentary unit I could  watch the BBC News as well at ten  o'clock at night with a keystroke I  could call up more than 150 websites  devoted exclusively to news information  I could read the New York Times from my  laptop at home before leaving for school  I could check out the box score from my  high school teams basketball game in the  local paper from the night before and  think of how that world has expanded  since 2001 so one of our opening  questions needs to be which universe  best serves the public and the place of  journalism in a free society as grateful  as I am to the founding fathers of  broadcast news for their vision there  are standards and their commitment to  the idea of news on the new medium of  television I have to conclude that this  new medium and this new universe is a  richer more accessible and far-reaching  cosmos of news information and  communication to be sure the world in  which we now live in this universe of  information is in considerable chaos  it's still in its formative stages  imperfect in its many elements I  described it as the second big bang  there are planets out there colliding  with one another trying to determine  which ones will support life some of  them are drifting too close to the Sun  and burning up others are forming common  cause as we engage them in a process of  review and definition recommendation and  implementation some historic context is  an order first the new universe is more  egalitarian in every way than in a days  when a handful of press the Lord's  pursue their personal political agendas  William Randolph Hearst Colonel Robert  McCormick of the Chicago  tribune the Chandler's in Los Angeles  Henry Luce at time inc were in  journalism not just for public service  but for profit and the fulfillment of  personal ideologies edward r murrow was  an icon of those who followed him but  Walter Winchell was at least as  persuasive if not more so to his  audience which was at least as large as  edward r murrow if not more so we lived  through the oj simpson trial in our time  in the funeral of Princess Diana  recently the funeral Michael Jackson the  Lindbergh kidnapping trial the Sam  Sheppard murder case and the Norwich of  Wallis Simpson to the Duke of Windsor  created the same climate of frenzy in  those years we may have known too much  about Bill Clinton's sex life but not  enough about John Kennedy's Sunday  morning now has become a regular  appointment for students of American  politics and policy well beyond what it  was in the era when only meet the press  was on for a half an hour with Face the  Nation even as a quantitative expansion  of the new universe is breathtaking in  scope it is the qualitative nature of  this new reality that draws us to this  occasion and others does it represent a  step forward and the unending quest to  know better the perils and the  possibilities of the precious time that  we have in this life or is it simply a  retreat to the lowest common  denominators of public interest and  exploitation the short answer it is all  of the above just as analysis restaurant  you can find just about anything you  want the new viewer is empowered as  never before to explore a wide range of  interests to personally determine his or  her daily informational needs and  curiosities but for that viewer and that  reader and for those of us on the other  side of the screen or in the printing  press the old order of trusting  credibility integrity and independence  requires a constant and vigorous  reexamination especially true given the  pressures of time and the meteor shower  information real and imagined and  personal and professional lives it is  under assault every hour because of the  simple fact the new order has a  voracious appetite for something too  often anything to just fill the time  that in turn has led to what can only be  described as mob journalism it is not an  entirely new phenomenon the gathering of  all the parts of the journalistic tribe  around an event manufactured or  spontaneous it is seldom however  reporting in the classic sense it is  more closely akin to day care it is a  live camera a warm body and an event any  kind of an event however banal that may  or may not lead to something meaningful  or entertaining preferably the latter so  eight years after I made those remarks  at Harvard what questions remain how do  we begin to answer the questions  especially when the business model what  we call the traditional media newspapers  in print broadcast network news weekly  news magazines when that condition is in  such a perilous state let us begin with  that as we work our way through truths  and myths about the current and future  place of journalism in a free society  and what can only be described as a  colossal miscalculation of the new  realities those of us in the traditional  media print and electronic allow the  canard that information is free we allow  that to take hold in the generations  coming of age with a dazzling array of  instruments that their fingerprints the  plucked information effortlessly out of  the air or so it seemed information is  not free reporting and gathering the  news getting it into a coherent and  engaging form is an expensive process  very expensive when wars break out in  remote locales such as a rock or  Afghanistan very expensive  there's a national or international  crisis requiring extra additions or  non-stop commercial free coverage myth  number two journalism is dead in America  journalism is not dead the business of  journalism is on life support but the  daily and long-form work of  Correspondence and editors photographers  and graphic artist that goes on every  day generally at a very high level of  excellence and importance I'm on two  committees that judge journalistic  competition one of the committee's takes  a look at the work of young reporters 35  and younger the other one recognizes the  long career a body of work those  journalists who become the foot soldiers  of our profession in both roles I am  annually seeing a wide range of stories  and series that hit the highest marks of  our profession long investigative pieces  about the bush administration's  manipulation of the rules on torture a  lengthy series and global warming that  range from the Arctic and Antarctica to  the remote reaches of the undeveloped  world a brilliant narrative on the mind  of an arsonist another on the victim of  methamphetamines that plague in rural  America and on and on this was not just  in one year this has been year in and  year out for a decade now national  public radio and its commitment to long  series and important issues wherever  they're reflecting lives in societies  has never been more popular in America  enterprise journalism is being practiced  every day and we ought not to lose sight  of that however I do not appear before  you as a dreamy and unrealistic promoter  of practices that are unsustainable  journalism is undergoing the most  profound change in challenges of my  lifetime great journalistic enterprises  and entrepreneurial individuals are  working feverishly on new approaches to  the business of journalism fully aware  that the old order of dominant cash  spigots is over  websites are ever more important in ever  more wide-ranging and imaginative in  both print and broadcast parent  companies of print and electronic  outlets are pushing the limits on  sharing costs and content in an effort  to reduce the expenses of covering the  news and getting it into form in which  it is useful to you investigative  journalist cooperatives are now being  formed there's talk of expanding that  concept to cover political policy and  congressional delegations as individual  paper struggle to maintain Washington or  state capitol bureaus the arrival of the  electronic book instrument the Kindle  from amazon or the sony electronic book  has generated visions of collectives of  media outlets selling those instruments  and a daily download of their products  for a fee maybe as much as a hundred and  fifty dollars a year or more I wish I  could say that I have for you a crystal  ball and then I know how all this will  turn out I don't and I don't it is as  perplexing a problem as I have  encountered since someone once asked me  how the television picture got from my  studio to their home I do know this as  journalists we have to be much more  proactive in the promotion of journalism  as a critical component of our  democratic society for too long it has  been we talk you listen or we print and  you read and maybe if we're so moved  will print one of your letters the  culture of journalism has been as  important to the evolution of this  unique society as the rule of law and we  cannot let it wither we need to remind  our readers and viewers our fellow  citizens of that fact we need to engage  them in the future of journalism in my  lifetime alone can you imagine America  going to war against Germany and Japan  without journalism to document the  reasons and the progress or the setbacks  the cold war in which M  harkins can make decisions about the  wisdom of US policies based on what they  learn from the public press as opposed  to the Russians who were hapless pawns  of men and cardboard suits and despotic  minds dr. King in the civil rights  movement depended on journalism to  document the cruel inequalities they  were trying to address Watergate without  Woodward and Bernstein it would just be  another hotel in Washington Vietnam 1968  gay rights and women's rights  affirmative action and the Reagan  Revolution all the yeasty and momentous  developments of our age were shared  documented analyzed and disseminated by  journalists often imperfectly but over  the long run impressively the place of  journalism in a free society is not the  obligation of journalists alone society  all of us must do our part as well in  this new and confusing environment  viewers and readers may need more help  than they've had in the past I have a  few suggested guidelines to begin as  viewers and readers we must be as  vigilant in determining our sources of  information as we are about buying say a  flat-screen television set or a mobile  phone or a box of granola just because a  statement lands on your laptop or PDA  wael website does not make that  statement true that same vigilant is  required of the traditional media as  well and remember this most of all most  bloggers are not journalists there are  freelance commentators unrestrained by  the facts or editorial context and  integrity moreover most appear to have a  distinct agenda to advance or personal  grudges to exploit other sources of  information and news assembled on a  small screen The Huffington Post and The  Daily Beast for example are partly  original but they're mostly aggregators  collectors of information  gathered at some expense and effort by  traditional media this is not to suggest  that I wish the blogosphere would go  away I am a free speech advocate and I  enjoy this new Tom Paine environment we  are a land of many voices and minds and  more need to be heard but we do have a  common obligation to measure and examine  what they're saying and not just take it  in and be aroused by it to young people  especially those who have not yet bumped  up against the realities of the hard  work of using information as a tool to  take on the many vexing problems of our  rural I have another set of guidelines  remember this you will not solve global  climate change by hitting backspace you  will not eliminate third-world poverty  by hitting delete life is not a cut and  paste proposition these new instruments  of communication research commerce and  information are transformational tools  they require your heart and your mind as  well as your fingers on the keyboard  they are carriers of information and  news but they do not originate those  critical components of dialogue and  decision-making among free people these  principles are neither stayed or toxic  they are critical to the health of the  profession and in the bond between the  viewer and the reader and news producers  they have not disappeared but their  place seems to have been diminished in  the daily struggle to master this new  universe moreover I very strongly  believe the place of my medium in all of  its forms is so pervasive and so  influential it has a fundamental  obligation to receive as well as the  send in the past I have participated in  town hall meetings on the press and  phoenix in pittsburgh in minneapolis and  other cities I've never failed to come  away with a better understand  learning about vital but fragile link  between my side of the screen and the  viewers after almost 50 years in this  profession small towns and the world  stage during times of constitutional  crises Wars natural disasters of epic  proportions social and economic upheaval  scientific triumph sand great personal  tragedies I have one enduring and  primary conclusion the people take us  seriously and we fulfill our obligation  to them and our place when we return the  favor they are empowered and so are we  by the riches of this new universe we  occupy we have gone past that memorable  19th century Chicago newspaper credo  print the news and raise hell well that  remains of stirring rallying cry the  fact is that we live in a far more  complex world of news and information as  this new world takes shape beneath our  feet and before our eyes on a daily  basis we cannot just randomly stumble  forward guided only by instincts for  that day survival but neither can we be  dismissive of the appetite of viewers  and readers for a rich variety of  sources engaging Lee presented whether  serious or trivial we owe it to  ourselves how we're calling our time and  place to raise to a higher station a  constant and wide-ranging dialogue on  the place and conduct of this profession  that is so vital to a free people and I  hope in some small way that this evening  will be a part of that necessary  colloquy thank you all very much
 

